<section class="card">
  <h2 class="card-title">loaded policy</h2>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p class="cell-dim">What the daemon has loaded and attached, zone by zone — not the policy source on disk. A <span class="mono">.fw</span> file that was edited and never reloaded reads exactly like one that is live, so nothing on this page comes from a file.</p>
    <div id="policy-table">
      {% include "fw/policy_table" %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2 class="card-title">policy-wide</h2>
  <div class="card-body">
    <table class="data-table">
      <tbody>
{% for f in features %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ f.label }}</td>
          <td class="cell-{{ f.semantic }}">{{ f.value }}</td>
        </tr>
{% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="card card-warn">
  <h2 class="card-title">the rules are not here, and this is why</h2>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p><strong>This page does not list the rules of the loaded policy, because the daemon cannot be asked for them.</strong> <span class="mono">fd</span> holds compiled BPF objects; the bundle manifest carries per-zone topology and no rule metadata, and the daemon has never seen the policy text. Anything a rule table here could show would have been re-derived from the files in the bundle directory — which is a claim about the disk, on a page whose whole claim is about the packet path.</p>
    <p>The rules as WRITTEN are <span class="mono">einheit-f show policy [&lt;zone&gt;]</span>, which reads the source and says so in its own output. Closing the gap needs both halves: per-zone rule metadata emitted into <span class="mono">manifest.json</span> by the compiler and captured at load, and a control opcode to serve it.</p>
  </div>
</section>
